"Release xxHash v0.8.4 · Cyan4973/xxHash · GitHub (https://github.com/Cyan4973/xxHash/releases/tag/v0.8.4)\nciteturn123view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn122view0\",\"lineno\":180}); Total lines: 264\nL99:     * AVAILABLE ADD-ONS\nL100:       * cite10†GitHub Advanced SecurityEnterprise-grade security features L101:       * cite57†Copilot for BusinessEnterprise-grade AI features L102:       * cite58†Premium SupportEnterprise-grade 24/7 support L103: \nL104:   * cite59†Pricing L105: \nL106: Search`/`\nL113: You signed in with another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You signed out in another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You switched accounts on another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. Dismiss alert\nL114: \nL115: cite61†Cyan4973 / cite62†xxHash Public\nL116: \nL117:   * cite63†Notifications You must be signed in to change notification settings\nL118:   * cite63†Fork 918 L119:   * cite63†Star L120:   * cite64†Code L121:   * cite65†Issues 7 L122:   * cite66†Pull requests 1 L123:   * cite67†Actions L124:   * cite68†Wiki L125:   * cite69†Security and quality 0 L126:   * cite70†Insights L127: \nL128: Additional navigation options\nL129: # xxHash v0.8.4\nL130: \nL131: cite71†Latest L132: \nL133: Compare\nL134: \nL135: # Choose a tag to compare\nL136: \nL137: ## Sorry, something went wrong.\nL138: \nL139: Filter\nL140: \nL141: Loading [Input]\nL142: \nL143: ## Sorry, something went wrong.\nL144: \nL145: ### Uh oh!\nL146: \nL147: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL148: ## No results found\nL149: \nL150: cite72†View all tags L151: \nL152: cite73†Image: @Cyan4973†avatars.githubusercontent.com cite61†Cyan4973 released this 19 Sep 00:32\nL153: \nL154: cite64†v0.8.4 L155: \nL156: cite74†`c87183a` L157: \nL158: This commit was created on GitHub.com and signed with GitHub’s verified signature.\nL159: \nL160: GPG key ID: B5690EEEBB952194\nL161: \nL162: Verified\nL163: \nL164: cite75†Learn about vigilant mode†docs.github.com .\nL165: xxHash `v0.8.4` is a general maintenance update, offering broader hardware acceleration, command line enhancements, and more predictable builds across platforms. No change to the API entry points.\nL166: ### Faster performance with vectorization\nL167: \nL168: This release adds several vectorized implementations: for the `RISC-V` Vector extension and LoongArch LASX, together with improvements to the existing LSX implementation.\nL169: \nL170: On `aarch64`, `XXH3` now prefers `NEON` over `SVE`. On tested processors, this improves performance by 25–40%. `XXH3` streaming performance has also been improved for workloads made of small updates.\nL171: ### Command-line enhancements\nL172: \nL173: `xxhsum` can now receive a full 64-bit seed through `-s` or `--seed`.\nL174: \nL175: Command-line parsing is more flexible: options may appear anywhere, even after filenames, while `--` explicitly terminates option processing.\nL176: \nL177: When processing a list of files, read errors no longer prevent subsequent files from being processed.\nL178: \nL179: Windows support received a substantial update, notably for long paths, UNC paths and filenames containing components that require extended `Win32` path handling.\nL180: ### Build and integration\nL181: \nL182: Runtime dispatch detection has been corrected for cross-compilation and non-x86 targets.\nL183: \nL184: `make` builds now isolate object files by configuration, avoiding accidental reuse across incompatible flag sets and allowing parallel test builds.\nL185: \nL186: The `cmake` integration moved from `cmake_unofficial` to `build/cmake`. Projects referring directly to the previous directory must update that path.\nL187: Low-level integrations can now redirect `XXH_memcpy()`, `XXH_memset()` and `XXH_memcmp()`. A new `XXH_NO_EXTERNC_GUARD` macro is also available for projects that need to manage C++ linkage themselves.\nL188: ### Correctness and portability\nL189: \nL190: A strict-aliasing issue that could cause `gcc` to miscompile some unaligned reads has been fixed. This release also addresses combinations involving `XXH_INLINE_ALL`, namespaces and x86 runtime dispatch, system headers included from an `extern \"C\"` block, and MSVC link-time optimization with warnings enabled as errors.\nL191: Portability improvements include compatibility with ARM builds using GCC versions older than 8, C++ compilation on Solaris and illumos, and improved installation metadata and paths on Windows and FreeBSD.\nL192: ### Detailed changelist\nL193:   * perf : added RISC-V Vector extension (RVV) implementation, by cite76†@zijianli1234 , cite77†@BoBoDai and cite78†@camel-cdr L194:   * perf : added LoongArch LASX implementation and improved LSX, by cite79†@24bit-xjkp L195:   * perf : prefer NEON over SVE on AArch64, improving XXH3 speed by 25–40%, by cite80†@Nicoshev L196:   * perf : improved XXH3 streaming speed for very small updates, suggested by cite81†@aleksazr L197:   * api : `XXH_memcpy()`, `XXH_memset()` and `XXH_memcmp()` can be redirected at compile time\nL198:   * api : new `XXH_NO_EXTERNC_GUARD` build macro for complex C++ integration\nL199:   * cli : added 64-bit seed support (`-s`, `--seed`), by cite82†@d06i L200:   * cli : options can appear after filenames; `--` terminates option parsing, cite83†#1098 reported by cite84†@unterkomplex L201:   * cli : continue processing subsequent files after read errors, cite85†#1064 reported by cite86†@ZoomRmc L202:   * cli : support long and special Windows paths, cite87†#1060 , based on cite88†#1061 by cite89†@t-mat L203:   * cli : improved benchmark accuracy, error handling and unit labels, cite90†#1051 reported by cite91†@calgray L204:   * fix : unaligned reads could be miscompiled by GCC under strict aliasing, cite92†#1013 by cite93†@lassipulkkinen L205:   * fix : `XXH_INLINE_ALL` compatibility with `XXH_NAMESPACE` and x86 runtime dispatch, by cite94†@pps83 , cite95†#1071 reported by cite96†@Dr-Hex L206:   * fix : avoid including system headers inside an `extern \"C\"` block, cite97†#1122 reported by cite98†@andreas-schwab L207:   * fix : compilation with MSVC link-time optimization and warnings as errors, cite99†#1038 reported by cite100†@mi2think L208:   * build: improved runtime dispatch detection for cross-compilation and non-x86 targets, with help from cite101†@eworm-de , cite102†#1081 reported by cite103†@PotMJ L209:   * build: `LIBXXH_DISPATCH=1` now applies to static and dynamic libraries, cite104†#1058 reported by cite105†@xiota L210:   * build: Make builds now isolate objects by configuration and support parallel test builds\nL211:   * build: moved CMake integration to `build/cmake` and improved cross-compilation detection\nL212:   * install: improved package metadata and installation paths on Windows and FreeBSD, by cite106†@iwamatsu , cite107†@chitao1234 and cite108†@sunpoet L213:   * portability: fixed compilation on ARM with GCC < 8, by cite109†@mstorsjo L214:   * portability: fixed C++ compilation on Solaris and illumos, by cite110†@luqmana L215:   * doc : expanded `xxhsum` documentation and clarified the algorithm specification files\nL216:   * doc : documented non-cryptographic collision properties, notably for XXH3's mid-size path, cite111†#1127 by cite112†@thomasahle L217: Full changelog: cite113†`v0.8.3...v0.8.4` L218: ## New Contributors\nL219:   * cite108†@sunpoet made their first contribution in cite114†#994 L220:   * cite79†@24bit-xjkp made their first contribution in cite115†#996 L221:   * cite116†@walterdejong made their first contribution in cite117†#993 L222:   * cite107†@chitao1234 made their first contribution in cite118†#1008 L223:   * cite119†@Schell-HAR made their first contribution in cite120†#1009 L224:   * cite121†@musvaage made their first contribution in cite122†#1021 L225:   * cite106†@iwamatsu made their first contribution in cite123†#1024 L226:   * cite80†@Nicoshev made their first contribution in cite124†#1023 L227:   * cite76†@zijianli1234 made their first contribution in cite125†#1043 L228:   * cite93†@lassipulkkinen made their first contribution in cite92†#1013 L229:   * cite126†@YexuanXiao made their first contribution in cite127†#1052 L230:   * cite101†@eworm-de made their first contribution in cite128†#1055 L231:   * cite129†@stefan-sedlak made their first contribution in cite130†#1068 L232:   * cite77†@BoBoDai made their first contribution in cite131†#1069 L233:   * cite78†@camel-cdr made their first contribution in cite132†#1066 L234:   * cite133†@FatihBAKIR made their first contribution in cite134†#1073 L235:   * cite110†@luqmana made their first contribution in cite135†#1093 L236:   * cite136†@marcusmueller made their first contribution in cite137†#1096 L237:   * cite109†@mstorsjo made their first contribution in cite138†#1092 L238:   * cite82†@d06i made their first contribution in cite139†#1088 L239:   * cite140†@NexusXe made their first contribution in cite141†#1101 L240: ### Contributors\nL241: \nL242:   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   * iwamatsu, mstorsjo, and 31 other contributors\nL243: \nL244: Assets 3\nL245: \nL246: Loading\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCollision ratio comparison · Cyan4973/xxHash Wiki · GitHub (https://github.com/Cyan4973/xxHash/wiki/Collision-ratio-comparison)\nciteturn123view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn122view1\",\"lineno\":170}); Total lines: 243\nL139: The test result indicates the expected nb of collisions for the amount of generated hash values. The tested hash algorithms do not have to produce a strictly identical nb of collisions. Being in the vicinity is good enough. For example, with 100G 64-bit hashes, the expected average amount of collisions is 312.5, but anything between about 280 and 350 collisions is statistically indistinguishable from expectation, so they are all \"good\", and can't be ranked solely on this information.\nL140: #### Testing 64-bit hashes on large inputs :\nL141: \nL142: An input of 256 bytes is presumed to always trigger the \"long\" mode for hash algorithms featuring multiple modes depending on input length.\nL143: Algorithm  | Input Len  | Nb Hashes  | Expected  | Nb Collisions  | Notes\nL144: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL145: XXH3  | 256  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 314  |\nL146: XXH64  | 256  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 294  |\nL147: XXH128 low 64-bit  | 256  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 314  |\nL148: XXH128 high 64-bit  | 512  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 291  |\nL149: blake2b low 64-bit  | 256  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 296  | very long test : 48h !\nL150: md5 low 64-bit  | 256  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 301  | very long test : 58h !\nL151: sha256 low 64  | 256  | 25 Gi  | 19.5  | 14  | very long test\nL152: city64  | 256  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 303  |\nL153: fnv64  | 256  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 303  | long test : 23h30, compared with 3h20 for `XXH3`\nL154: mmh64a  | 256  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 310  |\nL155: highwayhash64  | 256  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 321  | very long test : 35h !\nL156: t1ha2  | 256  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 761  | a bit too many, loses ~1bit of distribution\nL157: wyhash  | 256  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 1202  | a bit too many, loses ~2bits of distribution\nL158: mumv2  | 256  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 1229  | a bit too many, loses ~2bits of distribution\nL159: fletcher4  | 256  | 1 Gi  | 0.0  | 404908304  | is that a hash ?\nL160: The last part of the table contains hash algorithms with less-than-ideal collision probabilities. `t1ha2`'s distribution is closer to 63-bit, while `mumv2` and `wyhash` are closer to 62-bit. Such level of inefficiency is not \"horrible\", these algorithms can still be considered \"reasonable\" for many practical usages. Below that, on the other hand, listed algorithms should not be considered for any scenario.\nL161: #### Testing 64-bit non-portable hashes :\nL162: \nL163: `AES` hashes are based on Intel's `AES-NI` instruction extension, introduced alongside `SSE4.2`. These algorithms can't run on different platforms.\nL164: Algorithm  | Type  | Input Len  | Nb Hashes  | Expected  | Nb Collisions  | Notes\nL165: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL166: aquahash low64  | AES  | 256  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 3488136  | unusable\nL167: falkhash low64  | AES  | 256  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 18430  | not good\nL168: meowhash v0.4 low64  | AES  | 256  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 18200  | not good\nL169: ahash  | AES  | 256  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 758  | okay (loses -1.5 bit dist.)\nL170: meowhash v0.5 low64  | AES  | 256  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 300  | fine\nL171: `AES` implying cryptographic properties, one could believe that it's going to be good enough for simpler properties such as dispersion and randomness. This assumption appears incorrect, and `AES` do not offer any such guarantee \"by default\". The interesting Meowhash example shows that, with proper care, an `AES`-based hash can provide the expected amount of collisions as predicted by the birthday paradox.\nL172: But such outcome should not be considered \"trivial\" to achieve, and one would be well inspired to consult an encryption expert in order to produce a good quality hash using `AES` instructions.\nL173: #### Testing 64-bit hashes on small inputs :\nL174: \nL175: The test on input 8 bytes => 8 bytes output can check if there is some \"space reduction\" happening at this length. If there is no space reduction, then the transformation is a perfect bijection, which guarantees no collisions for 2 different inputs.\nL176: Algorithm  | Input Len  | Nb Hashes  | Expected  | Nb Collisions  | Notes\nL177: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL178: XXH3  | 8  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 0  | `XXH3` is bijective for `len==8`. It guarantees no collision.\nL179: XXH64  | 8  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 0  | `XXH64` is also bijective for `len==8`\nL180: mmh64a  | 8  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 0  | bijective\nL181: city64  | 8  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 278  | not bijective, but an acceptable collision ratio\nL182: mumv2  | 8  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 544  | a bit too large, loses ~1bit of distribution\nL183: t1ha2  | 8  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 607  | a bit too large, loses ~1bit of distribution\nL184: wyhash  | 8  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 652  | a bit too large, loses ~1bit of distribution\nL185: ahash  | 8  | 30 Gi  | 28.1  | 3841  | pretty bad, loses ~7bit of distribution\nL186: ##### Other lengths :\nL187: \nL188: Algorithm  | Input Len  | Nb Hashes  | Expected  | Nb Collisions  | Notes\nL189: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL190: XXH3  | 16  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 332  |\nL191: XXH3  | 32  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 287  |\nL192: XXH3  | 1024  | 100 Gi  | 312.5  | 300  |\nL193: ### Testing 128-bit hashes :\nL194: \nL195: The only acceptable score for these tests is always `0`. If the hash algorithm offers 128-bit of dispersion, the probability for a single collision to show up is smaller than winning the national lottery twice in a row. In contrast with the 64-bit tests, due to resource limitation, the test does not provide a precise `128-bit` collision estimation. It merely identifies algorithms with < 80-bit of effective distribution.\nL196: Algorithm  | Input Len  | Nb Hashes  | Expected  | Nb Collisions  | Notes\nL197: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL198: XXH128  | 256  | 100 Gi  | 0.0  | 0  |\nL199: cityhash128  | 256  | 100 Gi  | 0.0  | 0  |\nL200: t1ha2 128-bit  | 256  | 100 Gi  | 0.0  | 14  | distribution equivalent to ~70 bits\nL201: \nL202: Tests on small input lengths\nL203: Algorithm  | Input Len  | Nb Hashes  | Expected  | Nb Collisions  | Notes\nL204: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL205: XXH128  | 16  | 25 Gi  | 0.0  | 0  | range 9-16\nL206: XXH128  | 32  | 25 Gi  | 0.0  | 0  | range 17-128\nL207: XXH128  | 100  | 13 Gi  | 0.0  | 0  | range 17-128\nL208: XXH128  | 200  | 13 Gi  | 0.0  | 0  | range 129-240\nL209: XXH128  | 1024  | 100 Gi  | 0.0  | 0  |\nL210: \nL211: King converse\nL212: ## Wiki pages Pages 3\nL213: \nL214: [Input: Find a page or section…]\nL215: \nL216:   * Loading cite67†Home L217: \nL218: ### Uh oh!\nL219: \nL220: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL221: \nL222:   * Loading cite74†Collision ratio comparison L223: \nL224:     * cite75†Collision study L225:     * cite76†Testing 64-bit hashes on large inputs : L226:     * cite77†Testing 64-bit non-portable hashes : L227:     * cite78†Testing 64-bit hashes on small inputs : L228:     * cite79†Other lengths : L229:     * cite80†Testing 128-bit hashes : L230: \nL231:   * Loading cite81†Performance comparison L232: ### Uh oh!\nL233: \nL234: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL235: \nL236: cite82†Image: ietf-logo†private-user-images.githubusercontent.com L237: \nL238: ### Clone this wiki locally\nL239: \nL240: [Input]\nL241: \nL242: You can’t perform that action at this time.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPerformance comparison · Cyan4973/xxHash Wiki · GitHub (https://github.com/Cyan4973/xxHash/wiki/Performance-comparison)\nciteturn123view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn122view2\",\"lineno\":170}); Total lines: 247\nL120:   * cite62†Code L121:   * cite64†Issues 6 L122:   * cite65†Pull requests 0 L123:   * cite66†Actions L124:   * cite67†Wiki L125:   * cite68†Security and quality 0 L126:   * cite69†Insights L127: \nL128: Additional navigation options\nL129: # Performance comparison\nL130: \nL131: cite70†Jump to bottom cite71†Edit cite72†New page L132: \nL133: divinity76 edited this page May 9, 2024 · cite73†71 revisions L134: \nL135: ## Hash Algorithms - Performance comparison :\nL136: ### Bandwidth on large data (~100 KB)\nL137: \nL138: Platform : Intel i7-9700K, Ubuntu x64 20.04, `clang` v10.00, compilation flag `-O3` (all others default). Speed measured using open source program cite74†`benchHash` .\nL139: Hash Name  | Width  | Large Data Bandwidth (GB/s)  | Small Data Velocity  | Comment\nL140: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL141: XXH3 (SSE2)  | 64  | 31.5 GB/s  | 133.1  |\nL142: XXH128 (SSE2)  | 128  | 29.6 GB/s  | 118.1  |\nL143: RAM sequential read  | N/A  | 28.0 GB/s  | N/A  | For reference\nL144: City64  | 64  | 22.0 GB/s  | 76.6  |\nL145: T1ha2  | 64  | 22.0 GB/s  | 99.0  | Slightly worse cite75†collision ratio L146: City128  | 128  | 21.7 GB/s  | 57.7  |\nL147: XXH64  | 64  | 19.4 GB/s  | 71.0  |\nL148: SpookyHash  | 64  | 19.3 GB/s  | 53.2  |\nL149: Mum  | 64  | 18.0 GB/s  | 67.0  | Slightly worse cite75†collision ratio L150: XXH32  | 32  | 9.7 GB/s  | 71.9  |\nL151: City32  | 32  | 9.1 GB/s  | 66.0  |\nL152: SeaHash  | 64  | ~7.2 GB/s  | ???  | estimated from cite76†a different run L153: Murmur3  | 32  | 3.9 GB/s  | 56.1  |\nL154: SipHash  | 64  | 3.0 GB/s  | 43.2  |\nL155: Blake3 (SSE2)  | 256  | 2.4 GB/s  | 8.1  | Cryptographic\nL156: HighwayHash  | 64  | 1.4 GB/s  | 6.0  |\nL157: FNV64  | 64  | 1.2 GB/s  | 62.7  | Poor avalanche properties\nL158: Blake2  | 256  | 1.1 GB/s  | 5.1  | Cryptographic\nL159: Blake3 portable  | 256  | 1.0 GB/s  | 8.1  | Cryptographic\nL160: SHA1  | 160  | 0.8 GB/s  | 5.6  | No longer cryptographic (broken)\nL161: MD5  | 128  | 0.6 GB/s  | 7.8  | No longer cryptographic (broken)\nL162: SHA256  | 256  | 0.3 GB/s  | 3.2  | Cryptographic\nL163: Note : Small data velocity is a rough average of algorithm's efficiency for small data. For more accurate information, please refer to graphs in second part below.\nL164: ### Bandwidth of non-portable algorithms or implementations\nL165: \nL166: The following algorithms run on modern x64 cpus, but are either not portable, or their performance depend on the presence of a non-guaranteed instruction set (note: only `SSE2` is guaranteed on `x64`). Benchmark system is identical, but compilation flags are now `-O3 -mavx2`.\nL167: Hash Name  | Width  | Large Data Bandwidth (GB/s)  | Small Data Velocity  | Comment\nL168: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL169: XXH3 (AVX2)  | 64  | 59.4 GB/s  | 133.1  | AVX2 support (optional)\nL170: MeowHash (AES)  | 128  | 58.2 GB/s  | 52.5  | Requires x64 + hardware AES (not portable)\nL171: XXH128 (AVX2)  | 128  | 57.9 GB/s  | 118.1  | AVX2 support (optional)\nL172: CLHash  | 64  | 37.1 GB/s  | 58.1  | Requires PCLMUL intrinsics (not portable)\nL173: RAM sequential read  | N/A  | 28.0 GB/s  | N/A  | For reference\nL174: ahash (AES)  | 64  | 22.5 GB/s  | 107.2  | Hardware AES, results vary depending on instruction set and version\nL175: FarmHash  | 64  | 21.3 GB/s  | 71.9  | Results vary depending on instruction set\nL176: CRC32C (hw)  | 32  | 13.0 GB/s  | 57.9  | Requires SSE4.2\nL177: Blake3 (AVX2)  | 256  | 4.4 GB/s  | 8.1  | AVX2 support (optional)\nL178: `CRC32C` can be done in software, but performance becomes drastically slower.\nL179: \nL180: `XXH3` and `XXH128` and `Blake3` can also use `AVX512`, but this instruction set is not available on test platform.\nL181: \nL182: Note : some algorithms are effectively faster than system's RAM. Their full speed can only be leveraged when hashing data segments already present in cache.\nL183: ### Bandwidth on x64 with various data size of len 2^N\nL184: \nL185: cite77†Image: H_bandwidth_x64†user-images.githubusercontent.com L186: \nL187: It's interesting to note that the layers of caches are clearly visible in the graph. The host cpu has an L1 cache of 32 KB, L2 cache of 256 KB, and L3 cache of 12 MB. Performance of the `AVX2` variant visibly plummets on passing each of these thresholds.\nL188: ### Bandwidth on x86 with various data size of len 2^N (32-bit friendliness)\nL189: \nL190: So far, we have looked at 64-bit performance, where 64-bit hashes have an advantage, if only by virtue of ingesting data faster. The situation changes drastically on 32-bit systems, as emulating 64-bit arithmetic on 32-bit instruction set takes a big toll on performance.\nL191: The following graph shows how these hash algorithms fare in 32-bit mode. Most of them suffer a large decrease in performance. Only 32-bit hashes maintain their performance, like `XXH32`. But good news is, `XXH3` is also good on 32-bit cpus, and even more so when a vector unit is available.\nL192: \nL193: cite78†Image: H_bandwidth_x86 (1)†user-images.githubusercontent.com L194: ## Benchmarks concentrating on small data :\nL195: In many use cases, the generated hash value can be used to populate a hash table or a bloom filter. In these scenarios, it's frequent that the hash algorithm is just a part of a larger system, such as a database, and it will be invoked for a lot of rather small objects. When hashing small objects, hash properties can vary vastly compared to hashing \"large\" inputs. Some algorithms, for example, may have a long start or end stages, becoming dominant for small inputs.\nL196: The below tests try to capture these characteristics.\nL197: #### Throughput on small data of fixed length N\nL198: \nL199: Throughput tests try to generate as many hashes as possible. This is classically what is measured, because it's the easiest methodology for benchmark. However, it is only representative of \"batch mode\" scenarios, and also requires all inputs to feature exactly the same length. So it's effectively not that representative at all.\nL200: \nL201: cite79†Image: H_throughput_fixedS†user-images.githubusercontent.com L202: #### Throughput on small data of random length [1-N]\nL203: In this more advanced scenario, input length is not longer stable. This situation is generally more common, so the scenario is more likely to be representative of some real use case. However, being a throughput test, it's still targeting batch-mode scenarios, where a lot of hashes must be produced repetitively. In the graph below, length is effectively random, read from a generated series of value between 1 and N.\nL204: This situation is much harder on the branch predictor, which is likely to miss quite a few guesses, occasionally triggering a pipeline flush. Such negative impact can quickly dominate performance for small input. Some algorithm are better design to resist this side-effect.\nL205: cite80†Image: H_throughput_randomS†user-images.githubusercontent.com L206: #### Latency on small data of fixed length N\nL207: \nL208: Latency tests require the algorithm to complete one hash before starting the next one. This is generally more representative of situations where the hash is integrated into a larger algorithm. cite81†Image: H_latency_fixedS†user-images.githubusercontent.com L209: \nL210: #### Latency on small data of random length [1-N]\nL211: \nL212: cite82†Image: H_latency_randomS†user-images.githubusercontent.com L213: \nL214: King converse\nL215: ## Wiki pages Pages 3\nL216: \nL217: [Input: Find a page or section…]\nL218: \nL219:   * Loading cite67†Home L220: \nL221: ### Uh oh!\nL222: \nL223: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL224: \nL225:   * Loading cite83†Collision ratio comparison L226: ### Uh oh!\nL227: \nL228: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL229: \nL230:   * Loading cite84†Performance comparison L231:     * cite85†Hash Algorithms - Performance comparison : L232:     * cite86†Bandwidth on large data (~100 KB) L233:     * cite87†Bandwidth of non-portable algorithms or implementations L234:     * cite88†Bandwidth on x64 with various data size of len 2^N L235:     * cite89†Bandwidth on x86 with various data size of len 2^N (32-bit friendliness) L236:     * cite90†Benchmarks concentrating on small data : L237:     * cite91†Throughput on small data of fixed length N L238:     * cite92†Throughput on small data of random length [1-N] L239:     * cite93†Latency on small data of fixed length N L240:     * cite94†Latency on small data of random length [1-N] "
